Actually, I have observed that jousts do occur even when the lance skill is 
below expert level. I think that the probability of jousts increases with 
lance skill but jousting can happen (albeit less frequently), with a lower 
than expert lance skill.

> I think what confused me is exercising the weapon can happen
> either on foot or riding.

Right, attacking a monster in melee, on an adjacent square with a polearm 
(lance is a special kind of a polearm), while on foot does not exercise 
polearm skill. But the same does exercise polearm/lance skill when done 
riding and only lances can joust monsters.

> Two squares forward at unskilled (would only
> apply to a polearm for knights) or basic, using a knight's jump move if
> skilled, anywhere within two squares if expert.  That's plenty of hits
> before you can build up to a joust.  It rather matches with walking your
> horse much of the time until it grows into a war horse through its own
> combat experience, but riding it for the riding experience when
> traveling for distance.  Something like explore and clear a level on
> foot, move through a mostly cleared mostly explored level riding.
> Eventually riding more as it becomes necessary to increase riding
> skills.

That is pretty much the strategy I employ, let the pony grow up into a 
warhorse and then charge into war riding it. Although at deeper levels, the 
warhorse can be killed pretty easily by stronger monsters. Keeping the 
warhorse alive becomes quite difficult as you progress, especially if one 
does not have a stethoscope and/or a healing spell.

I usually advance the riding skill only to basic. At unskilled level, one 
cannot pickup items from the floor and has to unmount to do that. Except for 
knights, tameness decreases every time you mount your steed so that is 
generally not a good thing. Also dropping potions and other fragile items 
while riding at unskilled level causes them to shatter/break.
So the convenience afforded by advancing riding skill to basic is 
significant. Not sure if there is much benefit in advancing riding skill 
beyond basic level (I think I have read somewhere that it only gives a to-
hit bonus). Of course, knights start with riding skill at basic and hence do 
not face that particular problem.

